<BaseLayout title="O-Level English Tuition Singapore | Sec 1–4 | A-Worthy" description="O-Level English tuition for Sec 1–4, live online in groups of up to 6. In 2025, 90% of our O-Level English students scored A1–B3. Get a free assessment." ogImage="/images/og-english.png">
  <Header slot="header" />

  <nav class="sticky-toc" id="sticky-toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
    <span class="sticky-toc-eyebrow">On this page</span>
    <ul class="sticky-toc-list" id="sticky-toc-list"></ul>
  </nav>

  <!-- ===== PAGE HERO ===== -->
  <section class="prog-hero subject--english">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero-card prog-hero__card">
        <img class="sticker sticker--right prog-hero__sticker" src="/images/mascots/mascot-grade-a.webp" alt="" width="414" height="553" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
        <div class="prog-hero__copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">O-Level English</p>
          <h1 class="prog-hero__heading">O-Level <span class="accent-word">English</span> tuition<br />in Singapore</h1>
          <p class="prog-hero__sub">In 2025, 90% of our O-Level English students scored A1&ndash;B3. We teach with the SHARP Method, a five-step routine adapted from legal reasoning. Your child learns a named framework for every question type in Paper 1 (editing, situational and continuous writing), Paper 2 (comprehension and summary) and Paper 4 (oral).</p>
          <div class="prog-hero__actions" data-track="hero">
            <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-accent">Get a Free Report</a>
            <span class="prog-hero__fee">From SGD 280/month &middot; groups of up to 6</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== NAV PILLS ===== -->
  <nav class="prog-nav" aria-label="Programme quick links">
    <div class="container prog-nav__inner">
      <a href="#sec1-2" class="prog-nav__link">Sec 1&ndash;2</a>
      <a href="#sec3-4" class="prog-nav__link">Sec 3&ndash;4</a>
      <a href="#one-to-one" class="prog-nav__link">One-to-one</a>
      <a href="#schedule" class="prog-nav__link">Schedule</a>
      <a href="#faq" class="prog-nav__link">FAQ</a>
      <a href="#syllabus" class="prog-nav__link">Syllabus</a>
      <a href="#approach" class="prog-nav__link">Our approach</a>
      <a href="#frameworks" class="prog-nav__link">Frameworks</a>
      <a href="#playbook" class="prog-nav__link">SHARP playbook</a>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- ===== SEC 1-2 ENGLISH FOUNDATION ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="sec1-2">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Sec 1&ndash;2</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">A solid foundation, starting in Secondary 1</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">Start comprehension and writing work in Sec 1, and your child reaches the O-Level years with the basics already in place.</p>

      <div class="card-grid card-grid--1">
        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Foundation programme</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">One live lesson a week with the same tutor. Your child learns to read closely and infer, to plan a composition before writing it, and to fix the kinds of grammar error the editing section tests. We teach the same frameworks the O-Level class uses in the exam, so Sec 3 builds on what your child already knows. Their work comes back every week with written, marker-style feedback, and you get a progress report by email each month.</p>
          <ul class="prog-card__topics">
            <li>Close reading and inference techniques</li>
            <li>Essay structure (narrative, expository)</li>
            <li>Vocabulary, and using it in context</li>
            <li>Comprehension answering frameworks</li>
            <li>Grammar and editing accuracy</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="prog-card__meta">
            <span>90 min / week</span>
            <span>Max 6 students</span>
            <span>100% online</span>
          </div>
          <p class="prog-card__fee">SGD 280 / month</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== SEC 3-4 O-LEVEL INTENSIVE ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="sec3-4">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Sec 3&ndash;4</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">Sec 3&ndash;4: exam preparation, paper by paper</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">A mock paper every month, written marker-style feedback every week and a named method for each section of the paper.</p>

      <div class="card-grid card-grid--1">
        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">O-Level intensive programme</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Every week your child works on real past-paper questions and gets written comments on composition structure, grammar and comprehension technique. Once a month there&rsquo;s a mock paper, marked against the Cambridge band descriptors. Timed writing builds the stamina to finish Paper 1 on time. You get a progress report by email each month. Sec 4 classes end after the national exams, with no fees for the months after the last paper.</p>
          <ul class="prog-card__topics">
            <li>Paper 1 situational writing (purpose, audience, context, register, format)</li>
            <li>Paper 1 continuous writing (narrative, expository, argumentative)</li>
            <li>Paper 2 comprehension (literal, inferential, evaluative questions)</li>
            <li>Paper 2 summary (80-word paraphrase technique)</li>
            <li>Oral communication (video-based)</li>
            <li>Editing (grammar and vocabulary correction)</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="prog-card__meta">
            <span>90 min / week</span>
            <span>Max 6 students</span>
            <span>100% online</span>
          </div>
          <p class="prog-card__fee">SGD 320 / month</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== ONE-TO-ONE COACHING ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="one-to-one">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">One-to-one</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">One-to-one English coaching</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">For a student who needs individual attention or focused exam preparation.</p>

      <div class="card-grid card-grid--1">
        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Individual coaching</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Built around your child&rsquo;s own gaps, whether that&rsquo;s comprehension, composition, oral or editing. Lessons are on the A-Worthy Whiteboard, at times that suit you.</p>
          <div class="prog-card__meta">
            <span>60&ndash;90 min / session</span>
            <span>1 student</span>
            <span>100% online</span>
          </div>
          <p class="prog-card__fee">SGD 120 / session</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prog-inline-cta">
        <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-accent plausible-event-name=Subject+CTA+Click">Get a Free Report</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== CLASS SCHEDULE ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="schedule">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">Schedule</span>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">Class schedule</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">Every class is live on the A-Worthy Whiteboard, with at most 6 students. Lessons carry on through the school holidays and on public holidays.</p>

      <div class="schedule-table-wrap">
        <table class="schedule-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Programme</th>
              <th scope="col">Day &amp; time</th>
              <th scope="col">Duration</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Programme">Sec 1&ndash;2 Foundation</td>
              <td data-label="Day &amp; time">Saturday, 10:00 AM &ndash; 11:30 AM</td>
              <td data-label="Duration">90 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Programme">Sec 3&ndash;4 Intensive</td>
              <td data-label="Day &amp; time">Saturday, 2:00 PM &ndash; 3:30 PM</td>
              <td data-label="Duration">90 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Programme">Sunday Revision</td>
              <td data-label="Day &amp; time">Sunday, 10:00 AM &ndash; 11:30 AM</td>
              <td data-label="Duration">90 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Programme">One-to-one</td>
              <td data-label="Day &amp; time">Flexible, by arrangement</td>
              <td data-label="Duration">60&ndash;90 min</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="schedule-note"><a href="/contact/">Get a free assessment</a> and we&rsquo;ll suggest the class that fits.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== FAQ ===== -->
  <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">O-Level English tuition: parents&rsquo; questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-question">How much does O-Level English tuition cost in Singapore?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer"><p>At A-Worthy, SGD 280 a month for Sec 1&ndash;2 and SGD 320 for Sec 3&ndash;4, in groups of up to 6. That covers one 90-minute lesson a week and all our notes, practice papers and model answers, with no registration fee. If you&rsquo;re not satisfied after the first month of lessons, tell us within 7 days of that month&rsquo;s last lesson and we&rsquo;ll refund the first month&rsquo;s fees in full.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-question">How can my child score A1 in O-Level English?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer"><p>Your child needs marks in every section: editing, situational writing, continuous writing, comprehension, summary and oral. That comes down to method more than talent, and the SHARP Method gives them one: See the question type, Hit the right framework, Apply it, Refine through feedback, Practise from memory. At step H, each section of the paper gets its own named framework, from STAMP CARD for editing to CAPS for summary.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-question">Is English tuition necessary for O-Levels?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer"><p>Not for every child. But English counts towards the aggregate for JC and polytechnic admission, so a weak grade pulls down the whole score. A C6 or a B4 usually comes from weak technique, not weak knowledge, and technique can be taught. Priya K. went from C6 to A1 in five months.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-question">What is tested in O-Level English Paper 1?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer"><p>Paper 1 has three sections: editing (10 marks), situational writing (30 marks) and continuous writing (30 marks). We teach a framework for each: STAMP CARD for editing, the PAC analysis and four-move expansion for situational writing, and a story-spine plan or PEEL for continuous writing.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-question">When should I start O-Level English tuition?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer"><p>Sec 3 if you can, so your child has two years with the same frameworks before the exam. Sec 4 isn&rsquo;t too late: the class focuses on exam technique, and it ends after the national exams, with no fees for the months after the last paper.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-question">Can my child join mid-term?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer"><p>Yes. With groups of at most 6, a new student can join at any point in the term. The free assessment shows us your child&rsquo;s gaps first, and Jeremy plans the first lessons around them.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-question">What happens if my child misses a class?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer"><p>They get the recording. Every lesson is recorded on the A-Worthy Whiteboard, and a student who misses one receives the recording, that week&rsquo;s worksheet and the homework within 24 hours. There are no make-up lessons; the recording replaces them.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-question">Is online English tuition as effective as in-person?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer"><p>Yes, in a group of at most 6. Your child writes on a shared screen and Jeremy marks the work live. If your child is quiet, they can answer in the chat.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-question">Should my child continue into Sec 4?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer"><p>If the monthly reports show your child moving forward, yes. Sec 4 uses the same frameworks your child learned in Sec 3, with the same tutor, so the exam year is spent sharpening technique on real papers rather than starting over. The fee stays at SGD 320 a month, and the class ends after the O-Level papers, with no fees for the months after the last one. If you&rsquo;re unsure, or the reports aren&rsquo;t showing progress, message us on WhatsApp and we&rsquo;ll talk it through.</p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary class="faq-question">Can we stop or take a break?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer"><p>Yes. Give one month&rsquo;s notice to stop; there&rsquo;s no lock-in. Once a calendar year you can also hold your child&rsquo;s place for up to four consecutive weeks, with two weeks&rsquo; written notice, and pay nothing for the held weeks.</p></div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== SYLLABUS COVERAGE ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="syllabus">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">1184 syllabus</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">What we cover in the O-Level English syllabus (1184)</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">The written and oral papers of GCE O-Level English Language (1184), and what we teach for each.</p>

      <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">
        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Paper 1: editing and writing</h3>
          <ul class="prog-card__topics">
            <li>Editing (grammar and vocabulary correction)</li>
            <li>Situational writing (letters, emails, reports, speeches, proposals, articles)</li>
            <li>Continuous writing: narrative</li>
            <li>Continuous writing: expository</li>
            <li>Continuous writing: argumentative</li>
            <li>Planning, paragraphing, purpose, audience and register</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Paper 2 and Paper 4: comprehension and oral</h3>
          <ul class="prog-card__topics">
            <li>Visual text comprehension</li>
            <li>Narrative and non-fiction comprehension</li>
            <li>Literal, inferential and evaluative questions</li>
            <li>Summary writing (80-word paraphrase)</li>
            <li>Oral: the planned response</li>
            <li>Oral: the spoken interaction with the examiner</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== OUR APPROACH ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="approach">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">The SHARP Method</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">How the SHARP Method works in O-Level English</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">Jeremy Lim (LLB Hons, NUS Faculty of Law) built the SHARP Method from the way lawyers read and answer a problem, and applied it to O-Level English exam technique.</p>

      <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">
        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">See</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Many students lose marks before they write a word, because they misread the question. At the See step your child reads each question the way a lawyer reads a contract: the command word, the text type expected, the limits hidden in the rubric. Is a comprehension question literal, inferential or evaluative? Does the composition prompt want narrative, expository or argumentative writing, and in what register?</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Hit</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Once the question type is clear, your child picks the framework built for it, from STAMP CARD for editing to CAPS for summary. Each one follows the Cambridge mark scheme, so every step of the answer is aimed at a mark.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Apply</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Your child uses the framework to write their own answer to a real past-paper question: a full outline before a composition, FLIP or EIL answers where every sentence earns a mark. Jeremy gives feedback live on the shared screen, flagging a weak topic sentence, vague evidence or a missing content point as it happens, not a week later.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Refine &amp; Practise</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Your child checks each answer against a checklist for that paper: the right register, a reference to the text, a summary under 80 words with every point kept, tenses that hold. Then they rewrite key answers from memory until the framework is automatic. Practice spread across the term, not last-minute revision, is what carries the technique into the exam hall.</p>
        </div>


      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== QUESTION-TYPE FRAMEWORKS ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="frameworks">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Question-type frameworks</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">A named framework for every question type on the O-Level paper</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">&ldquo;Write better&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t advice a student can use in an exam. We teach your child which tool to use in each section of each paper, and how to use it.</p>

      <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">
        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Editing: STAMP CARD</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">The editing passage hides eight grammar errors, with two lines left clean on purpose. STAMP CARD is a nine-category sweep: Subject-verb agreement, Tense, Articles, Match pronouns, Prepositions, Connectors, Adjective/adverb word form, Right number and Degree of comparison. Your child checks every numbered line one category at a time, like stamping a bubble-tea card until all eight errors are caught. The three that turn up in almost every passage come first: Tense, Subject-verb agreement and Adjective/adverb. Checking this way catches the quiet errors fluent speakers miss, such as a tense shift mid-paragraph or &lsquo;less&rsquo; where &lsquo;fewer&rsquo; belongs. <a href="/frameworks/#stamp-card">See STAMP CARD worked, letter by letter &rarr;</a></p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Situational writing: PAC and the four-move expansion</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Paper 1 Section B is 30 marks: Task Fulfilment 10 and Language 20, for 250 to 350 words. Before writing, your child does the PAC analysis (Purpose, Audience, Context) and fixes the register in one word: formal, semi-formal or informal. Then they split every bullet into its separate instructions, because one missed point caps Task Fulfilment at Band 3. Each point then gets four moves: State it, Specify a detail from the visual text, Show what follows from it, and Sell it to the named reader. A short format checklist for each of the seven text types (formal and informal letter, email, report, speech, proposal, article) keeps the layout right; there is no separate format mark. <a href="/blog/situational-writing-guide/">Read the full situational writing guide &rarr;</a></p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Narrative composition: story spine</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">A Paper 1C narrative needs a story with a shape, not a string of events. Before writing, your child spends five minutes on four things: character (who is the protagonist, and what do they want?), arc (what conflict drives the story?), plot (setup, complication, resolution) and setting (where and when, and the mood it creates). The finished story has a clear turning point, sensory detail and an ending that doesn&rsquo;t feel rushed.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Expository and argumentative: PEEL</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">For Paper 1C expository and argumentative prompts, PEEL makes every body paragraph a complete unit. Point: a topic sentence that answers the question. Evidence: a specific example, statistic or real-world reference, not a generalisation. Explanation: two or three sentences on how the evidence supports the point. Link: a sentence back to the thesis that leads into the next paragraph. Most students can make a point and give an example; the marks are in the explanation.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <details class="prog-more">
        <summary class="btn btn--sm prog-more__toggle"><span class="prog-more__show">Show all 8 frameworks</span><span class="prog-more__hide">Hide the other 4 frameworks</span></summary>
        <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Comprehension: SWIFT &rarr; FLIP / EIL</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Students lose many comprehension marks by answering the wrong <em>kind</em> of question. SWIFT is a five-second test: if the question contains Suggest, Why, Impression, Feeling or Tone, the answer must be inferred (Category B); if not, it is already in the passage (Category A). Category A uses FLIP: Find the lines, Lift the words, Interpret them, Paraphrase only if asked. Category B uses EIL: name the Evidence, give its Implication in your own words, then Link back to exactly what the question asked. Sixteen named question types (A1&ndash;A8, B1&ndash;B8) sit under those two methods, so your child knows which one to use. <a href="/frameworks/#comprehension">Try the SWIFT classifier &rarr;</a></p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Visual text: ACED</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Paper 2 visual text questions ask your child to analyse an advertisement, poster or flyer. ACED gives four lenses. Audience: who is the text aimed at, and what words or images show it? Content: what claims or information does it give? Effect: what feeling or response is it after? Design: how do font, colour, image placement and white space serve its purpose? Your child learns to tie a specific design choice to a specific effect, instead of calling the colours &lsquo;eye-catching&rsquo;.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Summary: CAPS</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">The Paper 2 summary asks for about 80 words that keep every content point, and the language mark goes to answers in your child&rsquo;s own words, not lifted from the text. After numbering and sorting the points, your child rewrites them with the four CAPS moves. Combine &amp; condense: fuse related sentences, or replace a list with a category word. Alter word class: turn a noun into a verb and rebuild the sentence. rePurpose structure: reorder clauses or flip active/passive, without swapping any words. Swap words: use synonyms in the right register, never for technical terms or proper nouns. A two-part summary adds one transition sentence between the halves. We drill sixty graded items per technique until the moves are automatic. <a href="/frameworks/#caps">See CAPS with real before &amp; after &rarr;</a></p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Oral communication: PEEL and STAND</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Paper 4 is 30 marks in two parts (a planned two-minute response, then a live discussion with the examiner), and it rewards structure, not scripts. STAND opens every response in three moves: commit to a position in the first breath, say how many points are coming, and flag that the other side will get a fair hearing. Each point is then built in PEEL (Point, Evidence, Explanation, Link), so the reasoning stays clear under pressure. Your child also learns to widen an answer across the Spheres of Influence (Self, Family, School, Singapore, Global) instead of running out of things to say. We practise with recorded mock orals and play them back. <a href="#oral">See a real model answer, annotated &rarr;</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      </details>
      <div class="frameworks-cta">
        <a href="/frameworks/" class="btn btn-accent plausible-event-name=Frameworks+Explore"><span>Explore the frameworks in depth &rarr;</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

