          </div>
        ))}
        <p class="oral-anatomy__note">Point 2 (hawkers&rsquo; affordability and social role) continues in the same PEEL shape and is omitted here for length.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="oral-spheres">
        <div class="oral-spheres__head">
          <span class="oral-spheres__label">One prompt, five Spheres</span>
          <p class="oral-spheres__prompt">&ldquo;Should young people be concerned about protecting the environment? Why or why not?&rdquo;</p>
          <p class="oral-spheres__sub">The same view carries more weight as it widens. A strong answer doesn&rsquo;t need all five; two or three show range.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="oral-spheres__chain">
          {oralSpheres.map((s) => (
            <div class="oral-sphere">
              <span class="oral-sphere__name">{s.name}</span>
              <p class="oral-sphere__line">{s.line}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Inside the exam — from the PEEL Model Answer Scheme (video-based format) -->
      <div class="oral-exam">
        <div class="oral-exam__head">
          <span class="oral-exam__label">Inside the exam</span>
          <p class="oral-exam__facts"><strong>30 marks</strong> &middot; 20% of the subject grade &middot; ~20 minutes including 10 minutes of preparation &middot; both parts start from a short video clip. The clip is a <em>springboard</em>: examiners ask about the wider theme, never about the clip itself.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="oral-exam__parts">
          {oralParts.map((p) => (
            <div class="oral-part">
              <span class="oral-part__tag chip chip--blue">{p.part}</span>
              <span class="oral-part__name">{p.name}</span>
              <span class="oral-part__marks">{p.marks}</span>
              <p class="oral-part__desc">{p.desc}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="oral-band">
          <span class="oral-band__tag">What moves an answer up the bands</span>
          <p>The jump from Band 3 to Band 5 is the gap between an answer with <em>some development</em> and one that is <em>well-developed</em>. In Part 2, it is also the gap between speaking only when prompted and keeping a discussion going. In practice, that means the Explanation and the Link. <strong>Answers that stop after Point and Evidence are the most common reason for a Band 3 ceiling.</strong></p>
          <p class="oral-band__quote"><span class="oral-band__quote-tag">Band 5 &middot; 9&ndash;10 marks &middot; SEAB 1184, verbatim</span>&ldquo;A well-considered response which is well-developed and organised; uses a wide range of well-chosen vocabulary and structures.&rdquo; Every model in the scheme is written to satisfy this descriptor.</p>
