  <!-- ===== INSIDE A LESSON ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section lesson-flow" id="lesson-flow">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Inside a lesson</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">What 90 minutes at A-Worthy looks like</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__intro">Most parents never see inside a tuition lesson. Here&rsquo;s how a typical O-Level English lesson runs, from warm-up to homework.</p>

      <ol class="lesson-timeline">
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">0&ndash;5 min &middot; Retrieval warm-up</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Five quick questions on last week</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">Every lesson opens with five questions on last week&rsquo;s techniques: vocabulary, grammar or a quick inference. Students answer in the chat, and anyone who stumbles gets a one-line reminder before we move on.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">5&ndash;15 min &middot; Question of the week</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Read the question before you write</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">We put a real past-paper question on screen and do the <em>See</em> step together: the command word, the text type it expects, the framework we&rsquo;ll use. By minute fifteen, every student knows what a full-mark answer needs before writing a word.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">15&ndash;30 min &middot; Framework walkthrough</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Name the tool, then use the tool</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">FLIP/EIL, ACED, CAPS or STAMP CARD, whichever fits the day&rsquo;s focus. Jeremy shows a model answer, then writes a weak version on purpose so students can spot the gap. Because the tool has a name, students can reach for it again on exam day.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">30&ndash;60 min &middot; Live writing &amp; feedback</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Feedback while they write</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">Students write their answers on shared documents. Jeremy moves between them live and flags problems as they happen, before bad habits set in. This is where the small class matters: every student gets feedback from Jeremy at least three times in thirty minutes.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">60&ndash;80 min &middot; Peer marking</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Train the examiner&rsquo;s eye</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">Students swap answers and mark each other&rsquo;s work against the Cambridge band descriptors. Once they see what loses marks in someone else&rsquo;s essay, they start catching the same mistake in their own.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">06</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">80&ndash;90 min &middot; Exit ticket</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">No busywork goes home</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">A two-question exit ticket checks what stuck. Jeremy sets one focused practice piece, usually fifteen minutes of work, aimed at a specific weakness. It comes back with written, marker-style feedback, and your child can send questions on WhatsApp before the next lesson.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="lesson-flow__note">Every lesson is recorded. If your child misses one, the recording, worksheet and homework reach them within 24 hours.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

